  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }0.25{(1.1)}^{n}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{n}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n-1}}{{2}^{n}}$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }0.25{(1.1)}^{n}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{0.25{(1.1)}^{1}}{0.25{(1.1)}^{0}} \\\\  & =1.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n-1}}{{2}^{n}}$ starts with $n=2.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{{3}^{3-1}}{{2}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{3}^{2-1}}{{2}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{2}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3}{{2}^{2}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{2}^{3}})(\\frac{{2}^{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
